%U A399697 126781437824,40915795353344,4504286437888,3491368006912,106080016177664,184897606348032,615960506724096
%N A399697 Numerator of delta_1(n), the density of the integers having exactly one divisor in the open interval (n, 2n).
%C A399697 The set of integers having a divisor in the open interval (n, 2n) is a union of residue classes modulo L(n) = lcm(n+1, ..., 2n-1); hence delta(n) is a rational number, given by the finite inclusion-exclusion delta(n) = 1 - Sum_{S subset of {n+1..2n-1}} (-1)^|S|/lcm(S), the empty set contributing 1. The same holds for delta_r(n), the density of integers having exactly r divisors in (n, 2n).
%C A399697 Here delta(n) denotes the density of the integers having at least one divisor in (n,2n); delta(n) and delta_1(n) are the two functions of Erdős Problem 446. Besicovitch (1934) proved liminf delta(n) = 0; Erdős (1935) proved delta(n) = o(1); Erdős (1960) proved delta(n) = (log n)^(-alpha+o(1)), where alpha = 1 - (1 + log log 2)/log 2 = 0.0860713... is the Erdős-Tenenbaum-Ford constant (A074738); Tenenbaum (1984) refined this; and Ford (2008) determined the true order of magnitude, delta(n) asymptotic to 1/((log n)^alpha (log log n)^(3/2)).
%C A399697 Convention: the interval is open at both ends, as in Erdős's question. Replacing (n,2n) by (n,2n] or by [n,2n) changes every term but not the asymptotics, since the sets of admissible divisors differ by at most the single element 2n or n, so that the two densities differ by at most 1/n = o(delta(n)).
%C A399697 Erdős asked at Oberwolfach in 1986 whether delta_1(n) = o(delta(n)), writing that he was "quite sure" of it but that "recent results of Tenenbaum throw some doubt on this". Ford (2008) disproved it, showing more generally that delta_r(n) >>_r delta(n) for every fixed r, where delta_r(n) is the density of integers having exactly r divisors in (n, 2n).
%C A399697 The ratio delta_1(n)/delta(n) drifts downward as n grows, though not monotonically: it is 0.875 at n = 3, 0.6995 at n = 8, 0.6542 at n = 15 and 0.6430 at n = 28. By Ford's theorem it is nevertheless bounded away from 0.
%C A399697 Writing delta_1(n,m) for the density of integers having exactly one divisor in (n,m), this computation gives delta_1(3,6) = 7/20 = 0.35, delta_1(3,7) = 1/3 and delta_1(3,8) = 38/105 = 0.361904..., in exact agreement with the values 0.35, 0.33 and 0.3619 attributed to S. Cambie on the page of Erdős Problem 692.
%D A399697 A. S. Besicovitch, On the density of certain sequences of integers, J. London Math. Soc. 9 (1934), 2-6.
%D A399697 R. R. Hall and G. Tenenbaum, Divisors, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 90, Cambridge Univ. Press, 1988.
%H A399697 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A399697/b399697.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..40</a>
%H A399697 T. F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/446">Erdős Problem 446</a>
%H A399697 K. Ford, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0401223">The distribution of integers with a divisor in a given interval</a>, arXiv:math/0401223 [math.NT], 2004-2008.
%H A399697 G. Tenenbaum, <a href="http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CM_1984__51_2_243_0">Sur la probabilité qu'un entier possède un diviseur dans un intervalle donné</a>, Compositio Math. 51 (1984), 243-263.
%e A399697 delta_1(3) = 7/20: an integer has exactly one divisor in (3,6) = {4,5} when it is a multiple of 4 but not of 5 (density 1/4 - 1/20), or a multiple of 5 but not of 4 (density 1/5 - 1/20); in total 7/20, so a(3) = 7.
%e A399697 The fractions begin 0, 1/3, 7/20, 37/105, 73/252, 1172/3465, 1912/6435, 13294/45045, ...
%o A399697 (Python)
%o A399697 from fractions import Fraction
%o A399697 from math import lcm
%o A399697 def delta1(n):
%o A399697     D = list(range(n+1, 2*n)); tot = Fraction(0)
%o A399697     for b in D: # b = the unique divisor lying in (n,2n)
%o A399697         dp = {b: 1}
%o A399697         for e in D:
%o A399697             if e == b: continue
%o A399697             new = dict(dp)
%o A399697             for L, c in dp.items():
%o A399697                 new[lcm(L, e)] = new.get(lcm(L, e), 0) - c
%o A399697             dp = new
%o A399697         tot += sum(Fraction(c, L) for L, c in dp.items())
%o A399697     return tot
%o A399697 def a(n): return delta1(n).numerator
%Y A399697 Cf. A399698 (denominators).
%Y A399697 Cf. A399690 and A399691 (numerator and denominator of delta(n)), A074738 (the Erdős-Tenenbaum-Ford constant alpha), A397433 (tau^+(n), Erdős Problem 448), A399440 (r(n), Erdős Problem 449).
